# Logging

Every log line `[[ project_slug ]]` emits follows one schema, so Loki can filter to
exactly one provider, agent, session, content kind, queue, request or RBAC
decision.

## How it works

- Logs go to stderr as zap JSON. The cluster's Fluent Bit already ships them
  to Loki and parses the JSON fields (`Merge_Log On`), so no shipping
  configuration is needed.
- The helpers live in the `logging` package and emit each scope's fixed
  fields; call sites never type field names.
- Every line carries the low-cardinality `scope` and the `service`
  (`[[ project_slug ]]`).
- Field values are JSON fields, never Loki labels: session and agent ids are
  high-cardinality and must stay filterable as line fields.

## The schema

| `scope` | fields |
| -- | -- |
| `api` | `request_id`, `method`, `path`, `route`, `permission`, `status`, `user_sub`, `client_ip`, `duration_ms` |
| `rbac` | `subject`, `domain`, `object`, `action`, `decision` (`allow` or `deny`), `request_id` |
| `queue` | `queue`, `job_id`, `attempt`, `outcome` |
| `agent` | `provider`, `agent_id`, `session_id`, `kind` (`thought`, `tool_use`, `tool_result`, `message`, `prompt`, `question`), `content` |

- `api` is the access log: one line per request. The `request_id` is
  generated per request and returned to the caller in the `X-Request-Id`
  response header; `rbac` lines of the same request carry the same id, so
  the two pair in Loki. `path` is the request as sent and `route` the
  matched pattern, which is empty for a 404, so `path` is what an unmatched
  request has to go on. `client_ip` is the connection's peer; proxy headers
  are never trusted.
- `rbac` is one line per check of a route that is not public, allow or
  deny. `subject` is the Google account ID, and empty for a request with no
  session (answered 401). `domain` is `global` until routes are
  project-scoped. A route that needs only a signed-in user, such as `/me`,
  logs `object` `authenticated` and an empty `action`; any other route logs
  its permission's resource and verb, such as `Projects` and `Read`. `api`
  lines carry the signed-in user's `user_sub`.
- `queue` and `agent` helpers are defined and redacting; later phases fill
  them in against this contract rather than inventing one.
- `decision` is `allow` or `deny`, and `kind` is one of the six values
  above.

## Redaction

The `logging` helpers redact known secret values before a line is written,
because docs/roles.md says no role can read them and agent prompts and tool
results are where they would leak. Each secret value the logger is
configured with is replaced in an agent line's `content` with the literal
marker `[REDACTED]`. Redaction covers only values the logger knows, and only
`content`: other fields hold ids and names, not free text.

## LogQL

Every query selects the API's streams, parses the JSON line and then filters
on the scope's fields. Fluent Bit names its stream labels after the
Kubernetes metadata, so the namespace label is `kubernetes_namespace_name`
(not `namespace`), and pod labels appear with `.`, `/` and `-` turned into
`_`. `app_kubernetes_io_part_of="[[ project_slug ]]"` keeps out the Postgres and Redis
pods that share the namespace. The examples use dev (`[[ dev_namespace ]]`):

```logql
# api: failing requests, and requests that matched no route
{kubernetes_namespace_name="[[ dev_namespace ]]", app_kubernetes_io_part_of="[[ project_slug ]]"} | json | scope="api" | status="500"
{kubernetes_namespace_name="[[ dev_namespace ]]", app_kubernetes_io_part_of="[[ project_slug ]]"} | json | scope="api" | status="404"

# one request end to end: its api line and its rbac lines (no scope filter)
{kubernetes_namespace_name="[[ dev_namespace ]]", app_kubernetes_io_part_of="[[ project_slug ]]"} | json | request_id="0123456789abcdef"

# rbac: who was refused, on what
{kubernetes_namespace_name="[[ dev_namespace ]]", app_kubernetes_io_part_of="[[ project_slug ]]"} | json | scope="rbac" | decision="deny"
{kubernetes_namespace_name="[[ dev_namespace ]]", app_kubernetes_io_part_of="[[ project_slug ]]"} | json | scope="rbac" | decision="deny" | object="42"

# queue: failing work, by queue
{kubernetes_namespace_name="[[ dev_namespace ]]", app_kubernetes_io_part_of="[[ project_slug ]]"} | json | scope="queue" | outcome="failed"
{kubernetes_namespace_name="[[ dev_namespace ]]", app_kubernetes_io_part_of="[[ project_slug ]]"} | json | scope="queue" | queue="workflow-runs" | attempt="3"

# agent: one provider's tool results, one session's prompts, a word in content
{kubernetes_namespace_name="[[ dev_namespace ]]", app_kubernetes_io_part_of="[[ project_slug ]]"} | json | scope="agent" | provider="openai" | kind="tool_result"
{kubernetes_namespace_name="[[ dev_namespace ]]", app_kubernetes_io_part_of="[[ project_slug ]]"} | json | scope="agent" | session_id="session-1" | kind="prompt"
{kubernetes_namespace_name="[[ dev_namespace ]]", app_kubernetes_io_part_of="[[ project_slug ]]"} | json | scope="agent" | content=~"(?i).*migration.*"
```

## Dashboard

`deploy/grafana/[[ project_slug ]]-logs.json` is a Grafana dashboard over these
queries: line volume by scope, API requests by status, every matching line,
and one panel each for API 5xx and 404, RBAC denies, failed queue jobs and
agent lines. Its dropdowns are `namespace` plus the six contract fields
`scope`, `provider`, `agent_id`, `session_id`, `kind` and `request_id`, and
every panel applies all six, so setting `request_id` follows one request
through its api and rbac lines.

- `namespace` lists real values, because it is a Loki label. The six fields
  are JSON fields, and Grafana's Loki variables can only list label values,
  so their dropdowns offer the contract's fixed values (`scope`, `kind`) or
  only `All`, and take a typed value (for an id, paste it from a line or
  from the `X-Request-Id` header).
- The Grafana sidecar in `monitoring` loads any ConfigMap labelled
  `grafana_dashboard: "1"`. `deploy/grafana/kustomization.yaml` generates
  that ConfigMap from the JSON file, so the JSON is the only copy:

  ```sh
  kubectl apply -k deploy/grafana
  ```

- `deploy/grafana/dashboard_test.go` pins the six variables to the
  `logging` package's scopes and kinds, and checks every panel applies them.
